  "body_human_readable": "### Archimedes of Syracuse: A Biography of the Ancient World's Leading Scientist\n\nArchimedes of Syracuse (c. 287 \u2013 c. 212 BC) was an Ancient Greek mathematician, physicist, engineer, inventor, and astronomer, widely regarded as one of the leading scientists in classical antiquity and one of the greatest mathematicians of all time. Born in the city of Syracuse on the island of Sicily, he spent most of his life there, with a period of study in Alexandria, Egypt. His father was an astronomer named Phidias, and it is believed his family may have had noble ties, possibly being related to King Hiero II of Syracuse.\n\nFew details of his personal life are known, as a biography written by his friend Heracleides Lembus has been lost. However, his groundbreaking work in various fields has been preserved in his writings, which he often shared in the form of correspondence with other prominent mathematicians of his time. Archimedes's life was famously intertwined with his intellectual pursuits, to the extent that he would reportedly forget to eat or bathe while engrossed in his work. His life came to an end during the Siege of Syracuse, when he was killed by a Roman soldier despite orders from the Roman general, Marcus Claudius Marcellus, that he should not be harmed.\n\n### Major Contributions to Science and Mathematics\n\nArchimedes's legacy is marked by a vast array of discoveries and inventions that have had a lasting impact on numerous scientific fields.\n\n**In Mathematics:**\n\n*   **Calculus and Pi:** He anticipated modern calculus by using concepts like infinitesimals and the method of exhaustion to prove geometrical theorems. This allowed him to accurately calculate the area of a circle, the surface area and volume of a sphere, and the area under a parabola. He also derived a famously accurate approximation of the mathematical constant pi (\u03c0).\n*   **Large Numbers:** In his work *The Sand Reckoner*, Archimedes devised a system to express very large numbers, demonstrating that mathematics could represent numbers far greater than the grains of sand needed to fill the universe.\n*   **The Archimedean Spiral:** He defined and investigated the properties of the spiral that now bears his name.\n\n**In Physics and Engineering:**\n\n*   **Archimedes' Principle:** His most famous contribution to physics is the principle of buoyancy, known as Archimedes' Principle. It states that a body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces.\n*   **The Law of the Lever:** He provided a mathematical proof for the law of the lever and is credited with the famous quote, \"Give me a place to stand, and I shall move the Earth.\" He also developed fundamental theorems concerning the center of gravity of various shapes.\n*   **Inventions:** Archimedes was a prolific inventor. He is credited with designing the **Archimedes' screw**, a device for raising water that is still in use today. He also created compound pulleys, which allow heavy objects to be moved with less force, and is said to have developed an early version of an odometer to measure distance.\n*   **War Machines:** During the Roman siege of Syracuse, Archimedes designed innovative defensive war machines that long delayed the city's capture.\n\nHis profound contributions established him as a towering figure of the ancient world, and his work continues to be a cornerstone of mathematics and physics.",
